## Changelog:

Note on the version numbers: 1.00.00 - 1.26.08 are the historic culfw /
a-culfw line, the last of them from 2019. Since January 2026 the build runs
on PlatformIO and the third number is a build counter incremented by the CI
(1.26.100 and up), so it does not continue the 2019 series. Entries from
"1.26 (PlatformIO)" downwards belong to the old line.

#### 1.28
- Every device in culfw/Devices/ builds again with a current toolchain, and CI
  builds all 27 of them on every push. Only two did before: the rest tripped
  over -fno-common (GCC 10) and implicit function declarations (GCC 14), both
  now handled centrally in Devices/modern-gcc.mk.
- Device builds no longer inherit each other's object files. Sources from
  ../../clib were compiled into a directory all devices share, so building one
  device after another linked foreign objects — silently, and in CI it handed
  AVR objects to the ARM linker.
- CUL: the flash size check assumed a 2 KB bootloader where the part reserves
  4 KB, so it passed images reaching into the boot section. Corrected; the
  three CUL_V2 targets no longer fit against the real limit and are out of
  'make all'.
- CUL: SLIM_HM_BUILD, a HomeMatic-only profile. 15736 instead of 28202 bytes
  of flash and 992 instead of 2345 bytes of RAM on CUL_V3_868MHZ.
- CUL_V3: SBU UART (TTYSBU / PlatformIO env CUL_V3_SBU) — console over the SBU
  pins instead of USB, merged from feature/cul-v3-sbu.
- CUNO: would not link, .bss was 4394 bytes against the 4096 the atmega644p
  has. onewire.c held its 256-byte CRC table in RAM instead of PROGMEM (that
  alone saves 270 bytes on every device with HAS_ONEWIRE) and TTY_BUFSIZE went
  from 512 to 448.
- CUBe: the ARM unwind tables had no place in the linker scripts, so the
  bootloader did not link.
- ntp: own ntp_time_t instead of the platform's time_t, which newer newlib no
  longer declares through stdio.h — and which was signed where NTP needs
  unsigned.
- HmIP: the 'P' line is descrambled like the 'A' line. HmIP uses the same
  AskSin/BidCoS scrambling, so emitting it raw made this device and CULFW32
  print different bytes for the same frame on air.
- Build with a current avr-gcc: PlatformIO pins 7.3.0 instead of 5.4.0 (which
  builds smaller — CUL_V3 27354 instead of 27748 bytes), the makefiles use
  whatever is on the PATH, verified against 14.2.0.
- CI uploaded three committed Optiboot bootloaders instead of the firmware it
  had just built, on every run since January. Fixed.
- README described an ESP32 target that does not exist, and the CHANGELOG
  stopped in 2019. Both brought up to date.

#### 1.27 (2026-07-03)
- HmIP RX sniffer, merged into the AskSin receive task: 'Pr' / 'Px' switch a
  raw 'P<len><bytes><rssi>' line on and off. No separate module, no second
  FIFO drain.
- Length based BidCos/HmIP demux ahead of the descramble: below 10 dropped,
  10..17 BidCos only, 18..30 both, above 30 HmIP only.
- HAS_HMIP build toggle per device, enabled on CUL_V3. Sniffer only: no HmIP
  frame decoder, no TX, no AES-CCM and no pairing.
- Audit of the translation units active in CUL_V3: 1 critical (stack smash in
  kopp-fc), 5 medium, 13 low and 8 nit findings fixed.
- BASE_VERSION raised to 1.27. VERSION_1 / VERSION_2 stay at 1 / 26 on
  purpose: those two bytes are the EEPROM magic (fncollection.c), bumping
  them would reset every user's configuration on update.

#### 1.26 (2026-01-24, PlatformIO)
- Build system moved from the hand written makefiles to PlatformIO. Envs:
  CUL_V3, nanoCUL868, nanoCUL433, MapleCUNx4_W5100_BL, MapleCUNx4_W5500_BL.
  The makefiles are still in the tree and still build every other device.
- CI moved from GitLab to GitHub Actions.
- Build artifacts collected into /binaries together with a manifest.json.
- MapleCUN added.
- A native ESP32 port was started and removed again; a-culfw carries no ESP32
  target.

